schema_version: '1.0'
decision:
  action: improve
  weakness_id: W-PHANTOM-PRIMED-TRANSACTION
  failure_class: observation
  failure_mechanism: "incorrect_transaction_reconstruction + duplicated/lost transactions:\
    \ the monitor infers a transaction from a change of the sampled (data_in, code_out)\
    \ pin pair instead of from drive events. Because tb_top primes dut.data_in(=0)\
    \ at time 0, the idle pair (0,0) is X/Z-free, so the first sample is published\
    \ as a phantom 'settled transaction' before any stimulus; skipped/no-stimulus\
    \ tests therefore report '1 item checked, 1 passed'. The same pair-change rule\
    \ coalesces back-to-back identical drives (930 of 1000 randomized vectors scored)\
    \ \u2014 both weaknesses share this single root cause."
  source: mixed
  confidence: high
diagnosis:
  observed_problem: Every test whose scenario skips under the active elaboration (TC3/TC5/TC7/corner
    under the default DATA_WIDTH=4,SECDED=0 worker run) still reports '1 item checked,
    1 passed' because the monitor publishes the pre-stimulus primed (data_in=0, code_out=0)
    idle pair, and the scoreboard/coverage count it as a real transaction. Independently,
    identical re-driven vectors are coalesced, so the randomized test's scoreboard
    shows 930 items checked for 1000 driven vectors, contradicting the plan's 'scoreboard
    re-checks every driven vector' criterion.
  consequence: "The bench cannot distinguish 'stimulus ran and passed' from 'nothing\
    \ ran' \u2014 the phantom turns every adaptive-skip into a false pass (masking\
    \ W-ADAPTIVE-SKIP) and inflates/inflates check and coverage counts, so per-drive\
    \ exercise of the DUT is unverifiable from scoreboard/cov eports."
candidate_interventions:
- 'Monitor per-drive publication: add a drive counter to the shared HammingDutPins
  object (incremented by HammingDriver exactly once per item_done) and have HammingMonitor
  publish one HammingTransaction per counter increment, sampling the settle-boundary
  pair; the pre-stimulus idle sample is never published because the counter is still
  0. Fixes the phantom and the 930/1000 coalescing undercount together.'
- 'Seed-except-first in monitor.run_phase (lock on first X/Z-free sample without publishing).
  NOT safe standalone: TC1 drives exactly the zero vector, which equals the seeded
  idle pair, so the scoreboard would report 0 items and the report_phase empty-run
  guard would false-fail an applicable test; it would also require reworking the item-count
  guard.'
- Remove the dut.data_in.setimmediatevalue(0) priming in test_top.py. Only effective
  if the simulator presents undriven inputs as X/Z; Verilator's 2-state model may
  read 0 on undriven inputs, making the fix ineffective and non-deterministic across
  simulators.
- 'Defer / no_change: document coalescing as verification-equivalent (monitor docstring
  rationale). Rejected because the phantom actively masks the skip-weakness as a pass
  and violates the plan''s per-vector scoreboard claim.'
selected_intervention:
  type: 'TB-only observation fix: per-drive transaction publication via a shared drive
    counter (HammingDutPins.drive_count, incremented by HammingDriver after each item_done;
    HammingMonitor publishes one item per counter increment instead of on pin-pair
    change). No CONTRACT.md edit, no new ConfigDB keys, no new DUT ports, no RTL/spec/plan/model
    change.'
  expected_effect:
  - 'No phantom transaction: skipped/no-stimulus tests now report 0 scoreboard items
    and 0 coverage hits, so pass-by-skip is no longer masked as ''1 item checked,
    1 passed''; report_phase empty-run guard truthfully reflects exercised stimulus.'
  - Item counts match driven-vector counts for every test (TC1=1/1, TC2=1/1, TC4=16/16,
    TC5=256/256, TC6/TC8/corners per drive, HammingRandomizedTest=1000/1000), restoring
    the plan's 'scoreboard re-checks every driven vector' criterion and per-drive
    coverage sampling.
  - 'All 10 tests keep passing under the default elaboration: every driven vector
    remains bit-exact against hamming_encode, so no test outcome changes except honest
    counts.'
regression_risks:
- Publish timing can shift by up to one reference cycle when the driver's counter
  increment lands after the monitor's sample in the same boundary; for this latency-0
  combinational DUT the settled pair is held on the pins until the next drive boundary,
  so the captured (data_in, code_out) pair stays correct.
- Duplicate-driven vectors are now re-checked by the scoreboard and re-sampled by
  coverage, so numeric reports change (e.g., 1000 vs 930 in randomized); any downstream
  tooling that asserts exact item-count strings must tolerate the corrected counts.
- The publish rule no longer matches the changed-pair sentence in CONTRACT.md section
  10 (the contract FILE is not modified); this aligns the monitor with the higher-priority
  1:1 item rule of CONTRACT.md section 5 and the plan's per-vector scoreboard criterion.
- The new attribute on the shared HammingDutPins ConfigDB object must be incremented
  exactly once per item_done (mis-counting would over/under-publish); no new ConfigDB
  keys or DUT ports are introduced.
validation:
- Run the full 10-test regression under the default elaboration (DATA_WIDTH=4, SECDED=0);
  all 10 tests must still pass.
- Verify HammingTC3Test / HammingTC5Test / HammingTC7Test (inapplicable under the
  default elaboration) log 0 monitor publications / 0 scoreboard items instead of
  '1 item checked, 1 passed'.
- Verify HammingTC1Test reports exactly 1 scored item (the genuine zero drive) and
  HammingRandomizedTest reports 1000 scoreboard items checked / 1000 passed, matching
  RANDOM_VECTORS.
- Confirm merged coverage no longer shows phantom (zero, secded_off) hits from skipped
  tests and randomized bins are sampled per drive.
- 'Static check: python -m py_compile on the modified tb/driver.py, tb/monitor.py,
  tb/dut_helper.py.'

